@implements IDisposable
@inject AccountCharacterStore Store
@inject CharacterSession Session
@inject Accounts Accounts
@inject DiscardedCharacter Discarded

@* The characters this browser — or this account — is holding, and the way between them.

   **It replaced a panel called "Starting over", which was the first thing on the page.** That
   panel offered one destructive button and a link away to somebody else's finished character, which
   is a strange greeting for somebody who came here to build one. What belongs at the top is what
   you already have.

   **Starting another character and importing one keep the character on screen, and that is new.**
   Both used to write over it — "Start a new character" emptied the slot it was in, and an import
   overwrote whatever the current-character pointer was aimed at — so this browser could hold
   exactly one character however many the list was built to draw. Both now go through
   `AccountCharacterStore.StartAnotherAsync`, which writes the character down under its own id and
   moves the pointer before anything here touches the session. The rows above are the result.

   The ordering property that came across from the old panel still holds and still matters, only
   the other way up: **the keep lands before the session is emptied.** Setting the session off
   raises its change event, which starts a fire-and-forget write; emptying first races that write
   against the move and can put an empty sheet over the character being kept. `StartAgainTests`
   asserts the interop ordering, not just the outcome.

   **Discarding no longer asks first.** It used to open a confirm in the page whenever there was
   something to lose — the same two-step dialogue a browser `confirm()` is, only quieter. It now
   acts the moment it is clicked and leaves a single-level undo behind it instead, offered from
   the banner: see `CharacterSession.StartAgain`, `.ReplaceWithUndo` and `UndoTests`. A confirm
   pays its cost on every use; undo only pays on the uses that turn out to be mistakes. It is the
   one destructive control left on this panel.

   **The count is stated once, in `Aside`, not repeated in the closing sentence too.** It used to
   be both — "3 of 5" beside the title and "3 of 5 on this account" again at the foot — which is
   the same fact read twice on a panel with four other things competing for the same glance. The
   sentence at the foot now says only what `Aside` cannot: where a character actually lives. *@

<Panel Title="Your characters" Aside="@Aside" NoPrint="true">
    <ChildContent>

    @if (_characters.Count == 0)
    {
        @* **Says what to do, and where the character will actually be found**, rather than only
           that there is nothing yet — the same rule every other empty list in this app follows.
           The two branches used to be one sentence claiming "remembered in this browser" whoever
           was reading it, which was simply false for a signed-in account: nothing here has ever
           put an account's character in browser storage. *@
        <EmptyState>
            @if (_limit is not null)
            {
                <text>Nothing built yet. Start one below — it is kept on your account.</text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>Nothing built yet. Start one below — it is kept in this browser.</text>
            }
        </EmptyState>
    }
    else
    {
        <ChosenList>
            @foreach (var one in _characters)
            {
                <ChosenRow>
                    <Body>
                        @if (one.Id == _currentId)
                        {
                            <b>@one.Label</b>
                            <span class="small muted">open now</span>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            @one.Label
                        }
                    </Body>
                    <Actions>
                        @if (one.Id != _currentId)
                        {
                            <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="() => Open(one.Id)">Open</button>
                        }

                        @* No question first — see the comment on the panel above. Both kinds of
                           row are undoable from the banner the moment this fires: the one on
                           screen through the character's own buffer, any other row through
                           `DiscardedCharacter`, which reads it back before deleting it and can
                           write it to the same id again.

                           **The comment here used to say a different row "was never asked about
                           either, because switching away from it already left it saved under its
                           own id, safe from anything this button does".** The first half is true
                           of Open and the second half is not true of this: `DeleteAsync` is
                           exactly what destroys that copy, and nothing held it anywhere else. *@
                        <button type="button" class="btn danger" @onclick="() => Delete(one.Id)">
                            Discard
                        </button>
                    </Actions>
                </ChosenRow>
            }
        </ChosenList>
    }

    @* One row, one hierarchy: starting fresh is the reason anybody is on this page, and importing
       a character already made somewhere else is the thing a reader reaches for only sometimes —
       so it sits beside the primary action rather than in a field of its own below it, and it is
       drawn smaller. Loading a file in is here rather than on the review step because this is
       where somebody looks for a character they already have. *@
    <div class="inline spaced">
        @* "Start a new character" keeps its exact wording. It is what the old panel called this and
           what AreaTests asserts the tier page offers — and the act is the same act, so renaming it
           would cost a reader the only continuity they have.

           **No question first, and now nothing to ask about.** It used to empty the slot the
           character was in, so it was the most destructive control on the page and was drawn in
           the danger colour to say so. It now keeps that character under its own id and opens a
           fresh slot beside it — see `AccountCharacterStore.StartAnotherAsync` — so there is
           nothing to lose and nothing to warn about, and the red is gone with the danger. The
           reader's other characters are the rows above this button. *@
        <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="StartNew">Start a new character</button>

        <ImportCharacter OnImported="Imported" />
    </div>

    @* **A refused click has to say so, or it reads as a broken button.** Starting another
       character and importing one both keep what is on screen rather than writing over it, so
       when there is nowhere to put the new one they do nothing at all — and doing nothing is
       indistinguishable from a control that is not wired up. `role="status"` because this appears
       in response to a click and is the only report of it.

       It takes the place of the standing "account is full" line rather than sitting beside it:
       that line already says the same thing, and two sentences about the same cap under one
       button is the fact-read-twice this panel's own comment warns about. *@
    @if (_refusal is not null)
    {
        <p class="small muted spaced" role="status">@_refusal</p>
    }
    else if (_full)
    {
        <p class="small muted spaced" role="status">
            Your account is full. Discard one to make room; this one is still saved.
        </p>
    }

    <p class="small muted spaced">
        @if (_limit is not null)
        {
            <text>They follow you to any browser you sign in to.</text>
        }
        else
        {
            <text>These are in this browser only. Sign in and they follow you instead.</text>
        }
    </p>

    </ChildContent>
</Panel>

@code {
    private IReadOnlyList<SavedCharacterSummary> _characters = [];
    private string? _currentId;
    private int? _limit;
    private bool _full;

    /// <summary>
    /// What to say about the last refused click, or null when nothing has been refused. Cleared at
    /// the start of every attempt, so it never describes an act two clicks ago.
    /// </summary>
    private string? _refusal;

    private string Aside => _limit is { } limit ? $"{_characters.Count} of {limit}" : "In this browser";

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        Accounts.Changed += Reload;
        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Re-reads the list. Called on every change so the row marked "open now" is the one that is.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Refresh()
    {
        // Any re-read clears the last refusal. Discarding a row to make room is the obvious way a
        // reader answers "your account is full", and a message that outlived the thing it was
        // about would then be telling them it did not work.
        _refusal = null;

        var listed = await Store.ListAsync();

        _characters = listed.Characters;
        _limit = listed.Limit;
        _full = listed.Limit is not null && listed.IsFull;
        _currentId = await Store.CurrentIdAsync();
    }

    private void Reload() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        await Refresh();
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    private async Task Open(string id)
    {
        if (await Store.OpenAsync(id) is { } opened) Session.Restore(opened.Sheet, opened.Mode);

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Throws a character away, current or not — nothing here asks first, and both are undoable.
    ///
    /// <para>The one on screen goes through the session's own buffer, which
    /// <see cref="CharacterSession.StartAgain"/> arms. Any other row is read back <em>before</em>
    /// the delete and handed to <see cref="DiscardedCharacter"/>, which is the only thing that
    /// could put it back: the session has never held that character and the store copy is what
    /// this is about to remove.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Read with <c>ReadAsync</c> and not <c>OpenAsync</c>.</b> The latter moves the
    /// current-character pointer on the way past, so reading a row in order to remember it would
    /// switch the app to the character being discarded — and the next autosave would write the
    /// sheet on screen over the id just restored.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Delete(string id)
    {
        // **Order matters and is asserted.** Emptying the session raises its change event, which
        // fires a save nobody awaits; deleting first races that write. So the session is only
        // emptied when the character being deleted is the one on screen, and only after.
        if (id == _currentId)
        {
            Session.StartAgain();
            await Store.ClearAsync();
        }
        else
        {
            // Remembered first: after the delete there is nothing left to read.
            if (await Store.ReadAsync(id) is { } going)
            {
                var label = _characters.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Id == id)?.Label ?? "";
                await Discarded.RememberAsync(id, label, going.Sheet, going.Mode);
            }
            else
            {
                // Nothing readable to offer back — and the stale offer from a previous discard
                // must not be left standing over a row this one could not keep.
                Discarded.Forget();
            }

            await Store.DeleteAsync(id);
        }

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Keeps the character on screen and opens an empty one beside it.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It used to empty the slot instead, and that is the defect this closes.</b>
    /// <c>StartAgain</c> plus <c>ClearAsync</c> threw the character away — the owner imported one,
    /// pressed this, and lost it. Keeping it is <see cref="AccountCharacterStore.StartAnotherAsync"/>,
    /// which writes it down under its own id and moves the pointer before anything here empties the
    /// session.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>The session is emptied only once the keep has landed.</b> Emptying raises the
    /// session's change event, which starts a write nobody awaits; doing it first would race that
    /// write against the move and could put an empty sheet over the character being kept. On a
    /// refusal the session is not touched at all, so the character is still on screen and still
    /// where it was.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task StartNew()
    {
        if (await Keep() is false) return;

        Session.StartAgain();

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// An imported character goes into a slot of its own rather than over the one on screen.
    ///
    /// <para><b>It used to overwrite, which is half of what the owner reported.</b> The old call
    /// was <see cref="CharacterSession.ReplaceWithUndo"/>, whose whole purpose is the one-level
    /// undo that makes overwriting survivable — and it is the right call for the two things that
    /// really do replace the character on screen without moving the pointer, loading a sample and
    /// opening a recording. This no longer does: the character being left behind is written down
    /// under its own id first, so there is nothing to undo and an undo would only put a duplicate
    /// of it into the imported character's slot. <c>Restore</c> plus a notification is the honest
    /// pair — the same thing without the buffer.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Imported((CharacterSheet Sheet, SheetMode Mode) character)
    {
        if (await Keep() is false) return;

        Session.Restore(character.Sheet, character.Mode);
        Session.NotifyChanged();

        await Refresh();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Writes the character on screen down under its own id and opens a fresh slot, reporting in
    /// words when it could not. False means nothing moved and the caller must leave the session
    /// exactly as it is.
    ///
    /// <para>Shared by both controls above because they are the same act with different things put
    /// into the new slot, and because a refusal must read the same either way — two copies of this
    /// would be two chances for one of them to forget to say anything.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task<bool> Keep()
    {
        var outcome = await Store.StartAnotherAsync(Session.Sheet, Session.Mode);

        if (outcome == StartAnotherOutcome.Started) return true;

        // Refreshed first, then the reason written over what that cleared: a refusal is about the
        // list as it stands after the attempt, and re-reading it is also how a full account whose
        // cap has since changed stops claiming to be full.
        await Refresh();

        _refusal = outcome == StartAnotherOutcome.NoRoom
            ? "Your account is full. Discard one to make room; the character you have is still saved."
            : "Your character could not be saved just now, so nothing new was started. Try again in a moment.";

        return false;
    }

    public void Dispose() => Accounts.Changed -= Reload;
}
